# .dockerignore decides what "docker build" sends to the builder (the "build
# context"). Anything excluded here cannot end up in the image, even by
# accident, e.g. a careless "COPY . .".
#
# This is an ALLOWLIST, the same approach as AgentCrew: exclude everything,
# then re-include only the files the Dockerfile actually reads. A denylist
# ("exclude .env, venv, ...") protects only against files you remembered; a
# new .env.production or a stray key file would ship silently. With an
# allowlist, a file you forgot to allow makes COPY fail loudly at build time,
# which is the safe way to fail.
#
# It also keeps the context tiny. Without this file, "docker build" would
# upload ~1.85 GB from this folder before doing anything.
#
# Excluded by the first rule, among everything else:
#   .env / .env.*          API keys - these must only ever arrive at runtime
#   .streamlit/secrets.toml  Streamlit's own secrets file, same reason
#   venv/ .venv/           local virtualenv (~1.7 GB of Windows binaries that
#                          would not even run on Linux)
#   .git/                  full history, including anything ever committed
#   __pycache__/ *.pyc     stale bytecode compiled by your Windows Python
#   .pytest_cache/ .pytest-tmp/ .ruff_cache/   test and lint artifacts
#   chroma_db_*/ chroma_bench_*/ chroma_eval_*/  local vector stores (they
#                          hold the text of whatever you indexed)
#   *.log logs/            local logs
#   benchmark/ tests/ assets/ evaluate_*.py smoke_*.py README.md
#   .github/ .remember/ .claude/   not needed at runtime

# 1. Exclude everything.
*

# 2. Re-include what the Dockerfile COPYs. Later rules override earlier ones.
!requirements.txt
!constraints.txt
!app.py
!demo_key.py
!src/
!examples/
# Only the config file: .streamlit/secrets.toml must stay excluded.
!.streamlit/config.toml

# 3. Re-exclude junk that can live INSIDE the allowed paths. These come last
#    so they win over step 2.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.env
**/.env.*
